---
title: Strawberry Refrigerator Jam
description: Small-batch strawberry jam that sets on the fruit's own pectin and keeps in the fridge for 3 weeks.
tags: [jam, early-summer]
course: condiment
icons: [strawberry, jar, citrus]
difficulty: easy
source:
  name: Cook's Illustrated
  url: https://www.americastestkitchen.com/recipes/8434-strawberry-refrigerator-jam
yield: 2%cups
why:
  - A small batch of fruit cooks down quickly, and the less the fruit cooks, the fresher it tastes.
  - A shorter cook also keeps more of the fruit's own pectin, which the jam needs to gel.
  - Lemon juice and sugar help that pectin set firmly, so the jam needs no commercial pectin.
---

Place a #metal spoon{} in the freezer to chill.

Combine the @strawberries{1 1/2%lb}(hulled and cut into 1/2-inch pieces, 3 cups), @sugar{1%cup}, and @lemon juice{3%tbsp} in a #large saucepan{}. Bring to a boil over medium-high heat, then reduce the heat to medium.

Mash the fruit with a #potato masher{} until it is mostly broken down. Simmer vigorously until the fruit mixture thickens and a #spatula{} leaves a trail that does not fill in immediately, ~simmer{15-25%minutes}.

To test for proper thickness, remove the saucepan from the heat. Dip the chilled spoon into the jam and let it run off. The jam should slowly fall off the spoon in one thickened clump. If the jam is runny, return it to medium heat and simmer for ~simmer{2-4%minutes} before retesting.

Transfer the jam to a #jar{} and let cool completely. Cover with a tight-fitting lid and refrigerate.

> **Storing** — The jam keeps in the refrigerator for up to 3 weeks.

> **Serving** — Spread it on [pancakes](/recipes/pancakes/) or [popovers](/recipes/popovers/).
